  "notes": "Bronx Latin technician, and the one parameter the research calls out as unusual for the whole lane is accompaniment density: \"High note density in the accompaniment (unusual for the lane) to match the flow\". So `melody.densityPerBar` is the highest on this axis by a clear margin, and the montuno figure is what fills it — hence `arp` carrying half the countermelody weight rather than the answer-lick the rest of the axis uses. The salsa/mambo marker is authored as percussion and as bass anticipation, not as a mode: the research names timbale/bongo layers, a clave-adjacent 16th pattern and tumbao-style bass anticipation, and it names no scale, so inventing a Phrygian-dominant table here would have been fiction. ⚠ `medium` because the research rates it medium-high and says the workflow specifics come via the producers rather than from the artist. The genuinely inferred parameter is `swing` — 0.55 is chosen straighter than the boom-bap 0.58 default because a clave pattern does not survive MPC swing, and no source gives a number. ⚠ The research says \"Split personality\", so the two records are authored as `modes` rather than averaged: `capital punishment` is the minor album-cut half and `latin single` is the major-key interpolation half. Authoring modes replaces the boom-bap parent's dusty/jazzy/hard set, which is intended — those are not this artist's two records.",
